     {
      "type": "text",
      "text": "I've flagged the run as anomalous. The cause is somewhere in the instrument or process, but it's still undetermined: a seal leak or a blocked exhaust.\n\n- **What changed:** Tube pressure started growth at 3.045 Torr, matching the ramp baseline of about 3.04. It has gone up at every reading since, reaching 3.338 Torr at t=3240 s, about 10% higher.\n- **What rules out a flow problem:** Argon flow is steady at 99–101 sccm, so more gas isn't coming in. Temperature is holding at 750 °C with heater power steady around 46%.\n- **Why I can't narrow it down:** A leak and a blocked exhaust would both push pressure up like this. An exhaust oxygen reading would show whether air is getting in, but that sensor isn't installed. So the gas atmosphere during growth can't be confirmed.\n- **What would settle it:** Stop the argon and see whether pressure keeps rising (a leak check), or inspect the exhaust line and pump. Pressure data from normal runs at this point would also help.\n\nI've proposed that test, though the run continues regardless. No characterisation of the sample exists yet. If the cause isn't found, any result from this run shouldn't count as evidence about the recipe."
     }
